 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 2.02 — The Projective Plane\n\nEuclidean geometry has an irritating exception in it. *Any two distinct lines meet in exactly one point* — **unless** they are parallel, in which case they meet in none. Every theorem about intersecting lines needs a parallel case bolted on, and the exception is not deep: it is an artefact of the plane being slightly too small.\n\nThe projective plane $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ fixes this by adding exactly the missing points — one for each direction, where the parallels meet. The result removes the exception entirely:\n\n> **Any two distinct lines meet in exactly one point. No exceptions.**\n\nAnd the payoff is larger than tidiness. Once the exception is gone, a symmetry appears that was previously invisible:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Any two distinct **points** lie on exactly one **line** | |\n| Any two distinct **lines** meet in exactly one **point** | |\n\nThese two statements are now formally identical under swapping the words *point* and *line*. That is **duality**, and it means every projective theorem comes with a free dual theorem. We will see that the *same computation* — a cross product — performs both operations, which is about as concrete as a deep structural fact ever gets.\n\nThis notebook builds $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ from homogeneous coordinates, proves the meet/join statements computationally, and then verifies **Desargues' theorem**, whose proof in the projective setting is a determinant identity where the Euclidean proof is a case analysis.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. Building $\\mathbb{RP}^2$\n\nThere are three equivalent ways to say what the projective plane is, and it is worth holding all three at once.\n\n**As lines through the origin.** $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ is the set of lines through $0$ in $\\mathbb R^3$. Each such line is a \"point\" of the projective plane.\n\n**As a quotient.** $\\mathbb{RP}^2 = (\\mathbb R^3 \\setminus \\{0\\})/\\sim$, where $v \\sim \\lambda v$ for any $\\lambda \\ne 0$. We write the class of $(x,y,z)$ as $[x:y:z]$ — **homogeneous coordinates**. Only the ratios carry meaning, which is why the colons are used.\n\n**As the plane plus a horizon.** Split by whether $z = 0$:\n\n$$\\mathbb{RP}^2 = \\underbrace{\\{[x:y:1]\\}}_{\\textstyle \\cong\\, \\mathbb R^2} \\;\\sqcup\\; \\underbrace{\\{[x:y:0]\\}}_{\\textstyle \\text{the line at infinity}}$$\n\nAny point with $z \\ne 0$ can be scaled to $[x/z : y/z : 1]$, recovering an ordinary point of $\\mathbb R^2$. The leftovers, with $z = 0$, form one extra copy of $\\mathbb{RP}^1$ — a circle's worth of extra points, one for **each direction**. These are where parallel lines will meet.\n\nNote the third description is not symmetric: it singles out $z = 0$ as special. But that choice is arbitrary — we could equally have split on $x = 0$. **Nothing in $\\mathbb{RP}^2$ distinguishes the line at infinity from any other line.** That is precisely the content of 1.05's remark that affine geometry is projective geometry which has *remembered* which line is at infinity. Forgetting it is what we have just done.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\n/* Homogeneous coordinates.  A projective point is a triple up to scale. */\ncross3(a, b) := [ a[2]*b[3] - a[3]*b[2],\n                  a[3]*b[1] - a[1]*b[3],\n                  a[1]*b[2] - a[2]*b[1] ]$\n\n/* Scale a triple to a canonical form so we can compare classes.\n   Divide by the last nonzero entry; that makes representatives unique. */\nnormalise(v) := block([k, i],\n  k : 0,\n  for i : 1 thru 3 do if v[i] # 0 then k : i,\n  if k = 0 then error(\"the zero triple is not a projective point\"),\n  ratsimp( v / v[k] ) )$\n\n/* Same projective point?  Equal after normalising. */\nsame_point(u, v) := is( normalise(u) = normalise(v) )$\n\n/* Embedding of the affine plane, and the reverse */\nembed(p)  := [p[1], p[2], 1]$\nchart(v)  := if v[3] = 0 then \"at infinity\" else ratsimp([v[1]/v[3], v[2]/v[3]])$\n\nprint(\"affine (3,4) embeds as        \", embed([3,4]))$\nprint(\"[6:8:2] normalises to         \", normalise([6,8,2]),\n      \"   → affine \", chart([6,8,2]))$\nprint(\"[3:4:1] and [6:8:2] the same? \", same_point([3,4,1], [6,8,2]))$\nprint(\"[3:4:1] and [3:4:2] the same? \", same_point([3,4,1], [3,4,2]))$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"points with z = 0 are the line at infinity, one per direction:\")$\nprint(\"  direction (1,0) ↔ \", normalise([1,0,0]), \"   \", chart([1,0,0]))$\nprint(\"  direction (1,1) ↔ \", normalise([1,1,0]), \"   \", chart([1,1,0]))$\nprint(\"  and [2:2:0] is the same point as [1:1:0]: \", same_point([2,2,0],[1,1,0]))$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "affine (3,4) embeds as         [3,4,1]\n[6:8:2] normalises to          [3,4,1]    → affine  [3,4]\n[3:4:1] and [6:8:2] the same?  true\n[3:4:1] and [3:4:2] the same?  false\npoints with z = 0 are the line at infinity, one per direction:\n  direction (1,0) ↔  [1,0,0]     at infinity\n  direction (1,1) ↔  [1,1,0]     at infinity\n  and [2:2:0] is the same point as [1:1:0]:  true"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 2. Lines, and why the cross product does everything\n\nA line through the origin in $\\mathbb R^3$ is a projective *point*. A **plane** through the origin is a projective *line* — it is a one-parameter family of such lines. A plane through $0$ is cut out by a single equation\n\n$$ax + by + cz = 0,$$\n\nand the coefficient triple $(a,b,c)$ is determined only up to scale, exactly like a point. So:\n\n> **A projective line is also a point of $\\mathbb{RP}^2$** — it is $[a:b:c]$, the \"coordinates\" of the line.\n\nPoints and lines live in the *same* space, and the incidence relation is the symmetric condition\n\n$$P \\text{ lies on } \\ell \\iff a x + b y + c z = 0 \\iff P \\cdot \\ell = 0 .$$\n\nThere is nothing in that equation to say which triple is the point and which is the line.\n\n**Both constructions are the cross product.** Given two points $P, Q$, the line joining them must be orthogonal to both, so it is $P \\times Q$. Given two lines $\\ell, m$, the point where they meet must lie on both, so it is $\\ell \\times m$. One operation, two readings:\n\n$$\\text{join}(P,Q) = P \\times Q, \\qquad \\text{meet}(\\ell,m) = \\ell \\times m .$$\n\nAnd the cross product of two independent vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$ is never zero. That single algebraic fact is the whole reason the parallel exception disappears: **two distinct lines always meet, because $\\ell \\times m \\ne 0$.** When the lines happen to be parallel in the affine chart, the answer comes back with $z = 0$ — a point at infinity. Nothing special happens computationally; only the interpretation changes.\n\nNote also that this is the cross product from 1.01, doing a job with no metric in sight. There is no length or angle in $\\mathbb{RP}^2$; what survives is the *bilinear alternating* structure, which is the wedge product $\\Lambda^2$ in disguise, as warned in 1.01.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* One operation, two names */\njoin(P, Q) := cross3(P, Q)$    /* the line through two points */\nmeet(l, m) := cross3(l, m)$    /* the point where two lines cross */\n\nincident(P, l) := is( ratsimp(P . l) = 0 )$\n\n/* --- Two ordinary lines that cross at an ordinary point --- */\n/* y = x  is  x − y = 0  → [1,−1,0];   y = 2−x is x + y − 2 = 0 → [1,1,−2] */\nl1 : [1, -1, 0]$\nl2 : [1, 1, -2]$\nP  : meet(l1, l2)$\nprint(\"y = x  meets  y = 2 − x  at \", normalise(P), \" → affine \", chart(P))$\nprint(\"  on l1? \", incident(P, l1), \"   on l2? \", incident(P, l2))$\n\n/* --- Two PARALLEL lines.  No exception: they meet at infinity. --- */\n/* y = x is [1,−1,0];  y = x + 3 is x − y + 3 = 0 → [1,−1,3] */\nm1 : [1, -1, 0]$\nm2 : [1, -1, 3]$\nQ  : meet(m1, m2)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"y = x  meets  y = x + 3  at \", normalise(Q), \" → \", chart(Q))$\nprint(\"  on m1? \", incident(Q, m1), \"   on m2? \", incident(Q, m2))$\nprint(\"  the direction (1,1) — exactly the common direction of the two lines\")$\n\n/* --- Join: two points determine a line --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nA : embed([0,0])$  B : embed([1,2])$\nL : join(A, B)$\nprint(\"the line through (0,0) and (1,2) is \", normalise(L),\n      \"   i.e.  \", L[1], \"x + \", L[2], \"y + \", L[3], \" = 0\")$\nprint(\"  contains A? \", incident(A, L), \"   contains B? \", incident(B, L))$\n\n/* --- Every line meets the line at infinity exactly once --- */\nLinf : [0, 0, 1]$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"l1 meets the line at infinity at \", normalise(meet(l1, Linf)))$\nprint(\"l2 meets the line at infinity at \", normalise(meet(l2, Linf)))$\nprint(\"parallel lines share that point; non-parallel ones do not.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "y = x  meets  y = 2 − x  at  [1,1,1]  → affine  [1,1]\n  on l1?  true    on l2?  true\ny = x  meets  y = x + 3  at  [1,1,0]  →  at infinity\n  on m1?  true    on m2?  true\n  the direction (1,1) — exactly the common direction of the two lines\nthe line through (0,0) and (1,2) is  [-2,1,0]    i.e.   -2 x +  1 y +  0  = 0\n  contains A?  true    contains B?  true\nl1 meets the line at infinity at  [1,1,0]\nl2 meets the line at infinity at  [-1,1,0]\nparallel lines share that point; non-parallel ones do not."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 3. Duality\n\nBecause points and lines are both triples, and incidence $P \\cdot \\ell = 0$ is symmetric in them, the projective plane admits an exact symmetry:\n\n> **Duality principle.** In any true statement of projective geometry, interchange the words *point* and *line* (and correspondingly *lies on* and *passes through*, *collinear* and *concurrent*, *join* and *meet*). The result is another true statement.\n\nThis is not an analogy — it is a theorem, and the proof is the observation we have just made computationally: the axioms are symmetric under the swap, so any consequence of them is too. Every projective theorem is really two theorems.\n\n| Statement | Dual statement |\n|---|---|\n| two distinct points lie on a unique line | two distinct lines meet in a unique point |\n| three points are collinear | three lines are concurrent |\n| $\\det(P_1, P_2, P_3) = 0$ | $\\det(\\ell_1, \\ell_2, \\ell_3) = 0$ |\n| Pascal's theorem (hexagon in a conic) | Brianchon's theorem (hexagon around a conic) |\n\nThe third row is the one we already used without comment: in 1.05, Ceva and Menelaus turned out to be the vanishing of two determinants that differed only in whether the rows were lines or points. **They are dual theorems**, which is why they looked so similar and why the products differed only in sign. That observation, made as a remark at the end of Stage 1, is now a consequence of a principle.\n\nDuality also explains an economy in the code below: there is no separate `meet` and `join` routine, because there is no separate operation.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "det3(a, b, c) := determinant(matrix(a, b, c))$\n\ncollinear(P, Q, R) := is( ratsimp(det3(P, Q, R)) = 0 )$\nconcurrent(l, m, n):= is( ratsimp(det3(l, m, n)) = 0 )$   /* the SAME test */\n\n/* Three points on a line, and the dual: three lines through a point */\nA : [1, 0, 1]$  B : [0, 1, 1]$  C : [2, -1, 1]$\nprint(\"A, B, C collinear? \", collinear(A, B, C),\n      \"    (C = 2A − B, so yes)\")$\n\n/* Dualise: treat the same three triples as lines */\nprint(\"the same triples read as lines are concurrent? \", concurrent(A, B, C))$\nprint(\"meeting at \", normalise(meet(A, B)))$\nprint(\"  — one determinant, two theorems\")$\n\n/* Ceva and Menelaus from 1.05, revisited as duals.\n   The cevian lines were built by join; the transversal points by meet. */\nprint(\"\")$\nAb : [1,0,0]$  Bb : [0,1,0]$  Cb : [0,0,1]$\nD : [0, 1-t, t]$   E : [u, 0, 1-u]$   F : [1-v, v, 0]$\nprint(\"Ceva     — det of the three JOINED lines : \",\n      expand( det3(join(Ab,D), join(Bb,E), join(Cb,F)) ))$\nprint(\"Menelaus — det of the three POINTS       : \",\n      expand( det3(D, E, F) ))$\nprint(\"dual constructions, and the two conditions differ only in a sign\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "A, B, C collinear?  true     (C = 2A − B, so yes)\nthe same triples read as lines are concurrent?  true\nmeeting at  [-1,-1,1]\n  — one determinant, two theorems\nCeva     — det of the three JOINED lines :  -(2*t*u*v)+u*v+t*v-v+t*u-u-t+1\nMenelaus — det of the three POINTS       :  u*v+t*v-v+t*u-u-t+1\ndual constructions, and the two conditions differ only in a sign"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 4. Desargues' theorem\n\n> **Desargues.** Two triangles are in perspective **from a point** if and only if they are in perspective **from a line**.\n\nUnpacked: if the lines $AA'$, $BB'$, $CC'$ all pass through a common point $O$, then the three intersection points of corresponding sides,\n\n$$P = AB \\cap A'B', \\qquad Q = BC \\cap B'C', \\qquad R = CA \\cap C'A',$$\n\nare collinear — and conversely.\n\nThe statement is **self-dual**: swapping point and line turns \"perspective from a point\" into \"perspective from a line\", so the theorem is its own dual and the converse comes free.\n\nIn Euclidean geometry this is a genuinely awkward theorem, because any of the intersections may fail to exist when sides happen to be parallel, and the proof splinters into cases. Projectively there are no cases: every intersection exists, and the whole thing reduces to a determinant vanishing.\n\n**Why we may normalise.** $PGL(3)$ acts transitively on ordered 4-tuples of points in general position (this is proved in 2.03). So we lose no generality by placing\n\n$$A = [1{:}0{:}0], \\quad B = [0{:}1{:}0], \\quad C = [0{:}0{:}1], \\quad O = [1{:}1{:}1],$$\n\nand letting $A' = O + aA$, $B' = O + bB$, $C' = O + cC$ range over the lines through $O$ with $a, b, c$ free. That is a complete proof, not a special case — and it keeps the algebra small enough to read. We spot-check afterwards with an unnormalised configuration.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Desargues, in the normalised frame --- */\nAd : [1,0,0]$  Bd : [0,1,0]$  Cd : [0,0,1]$  Od : [1,1,1]$\n\n/* A' lies on the line OA, and similarly for B', C' */\nAp : Od + a*Ad$\nBp : Od + b*Bd$\nCp : Od + c*Cd$\nprint(\"A' = \", Ap, \"   B' = \", Bp, \"   C' = \", Cp)$\n\n/* check they really are in perspective from O */\nprint(\"O on AA'? \", incident(Od, join(Ad, Ap)),\n      \"   O on BB'? \", incident(Od, join(Bd, Bp)),\n      \"   O on CC'? \", incident(Od, join(Cd, Cp)))$\n\n/* The three intersections of corresponding sides */\nPd : meet( join(Ad, Bd), join(Ap, Bp) )$\nQd : meet( join(Bd, Cd), join(Bp, Cp) )$\nRd : meet( join(Cd, Ad), join(Cp, Ap) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"P = AB ∩ A'B' = \", ratsimp(Pd))$\nprint(\"Q = BC ∩ B'C' = \", ratsimp(Qd))$\nprint(\"R = CA ∩ C'A' = \", ratsimp(Rd))$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"det[P,Q,R] = \", ratsimp( det3(Pd, Qd, Rd) ), \"   ⟹ P, Q, R are collinear\")$\n\n/* The axis of perspective — the line all three lie on */\naxis : ratsimp( join(Pd, Qd) )$\nprint(\"axis of perspective = \", axis)$\nprint(\"R lies on it? \", is( ratsimp(Rd . axis) = 0 ))$\n\n/* --- Spot-check on an unnormalised configuration --- */\n/* Deliberately ugly coordinates, and different multipliers on each line. */\nO2 : [2, -3, 5]$  A2 : [1, 4, 1]$  B2 : [-2, 1, 3]$  C2 : [7, 0, -1]$\nA2p : 3*O2 + 2*A2$   B2p : -1*O2 + 5*B2$   C2p : 4*O2 - 3*C2$\nP2 : meet( join(A2, B2), join(A2p, B2p) )$\nQ2 : meet( join(B2, C2), join(B2p, C2p) )$\nR2 : meet( join(C2, A2), join(C2p, A2p) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"unnormalised check — P, Q, R = \", P2, \", \", Q2, \", \", R2)$\nprint(\"det[P,Q,R] = \", ratsimp(det3(P2, Q2, R2)), \"   collinear ✓\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 5,
   "outputs": [
