   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 5. What kind of surface is $\\mathbb{RP}^2$?\n\nWorth pausing on, because the answer is strange. Take the description \"lines through the origin in $\\mathbb R^3$\" and intersect each line with the unit sphere: every line meets $S^2$ in **two** antipodal points. So\n\n$$\\mathbb{RP}^2 = S^2/(v \\sim -v),$$\n\nthe sphere with antipodal points identified. Equivalently, take the upper hemisphere (a disc) and glue each boundary point to its antipode.\n\nThe consequences are not what Euclidean intuition expects:\n\n- **It is compact** — no edge, finite extent, though it contains a copy of the whole infinite plane.\n- **It is non-orientable.** A figure transported around a loop crossing the line at infinity comes back mirror-reversed. There is no consistent notion of \"clockwise\", so the chirality of 1.07 has no projective meaning at all.\n- **It does not embed in $\\mathbb R^3$** without self-intersection. Pictures of it (Boy's surface, the cross-cap) all cheat.\n\nThis is a genuine warning about the pictures we have been drawing. Every diagram in this notebook lives in an affine chart — the part where $z \\ne 0$ — and the chart is a proper subset. The line at infinity is invisible in the picture but not in the space, and the two triangles of the Desargues figure could equally well have had their axis of perspective *at* infinity, in which case the affine picture shows three pairs of parallel sides and no visible axis at all. Same theorem, same computation, different chart.\n\ntopology-journey Stage 1.06 constructs $\\mathbb{RP}^n$ as a quotient space and Stage 2 computes its fundamental group, $\\pi_1(\\mathbb{RP}^2) = \\mathbb Z/2$ — the algebraic statement of \"go round twice to get home\", which is the same $\\mathbb Z/2$ we met as the double cover $S^3 \\to SO(3)$ in 1.03. In fact $\\mathbb{RP}^3 \\cong SO(3)$ exactly.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Object | Homogeneous form | Operation |\n|---|---|---|\n| point | $[x:y:z]$, up to scale | — |\n| line | $[a:b:c]$, up to scale | — |\n| incidence | $P \\cdot \\ell = 0$ | symmetric in $P$ and $\\ell$ |\n| line through two points | $P \\times Q$ | join |\n| point on two lines | $\\ell \\times m$ | meet |\n| collinear / concurrent | $\\det = 0$ | the same test |\n\nThree things were achieved:\n\n1. **The parallel exception vanished.** Two distinct lines always meet, because the cross product of independent vectors is never zero. Parallel lines meet at a point with $z = 0$.\n2. **Duality became a theorem.** Points and lines are the same kind of object, so every projective statement has a dual — which retroactively explains why Ceva and Menelaus were twins.\n3. **Desargues became a one-line computation**, valid without case analysis, where the Euclidean proof needs a separate argument for every parallel configuration.\n\n## Next\n\n**2.03 — Cross-Ratio and Projectivities** introduces $PGL(3)$ properly, proves the transitivity on 4-point frames that we assumed when normalising the Desargues configuration, and identifies the cross-ratio as *the* projective invariant — the one survivor from 2.01's table.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Stillwell, J. (2005) *The Four Pillars of Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/0-387-29052-4. Chapter 5 gives the axioms of a projective plane and Chapter 6 treats Pappus and Desargues as incidence theorems — the closest match to this notebook.\n- ★ Hartshorne, R. (2000) *Geometry: Euclid and Beyond*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-0-387-22676-7. Desargues appears at §14 (Abstract Fields and Incidence, p. 133) and again as Exercise 20.8, proved synthetically over a field rather than with homogeneous coordinates — a useful contrast with the determinant proof given here. Note this book has no projective-geometry chapter as such.\n- ★ Martin, G.E. (1982) *Transformation Geometry: An Introduction to Symmetry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4612-5680-9. Includes the historical note that Desargues' work lay dormant until Poncelet revived it in 1822.\n- Richter-Gebert, J. (2011) *Perspectives on Projective Geometry: A Guided Tour Through Real and Complex Geometry*. Berlin: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-642-17286-1. The modern computational treatment; the cross-product formulation of meet and join used here is developed at length in its early chapters.\n- Coxeter, H.S.M. (1987) *Projective Geometry*. 2nd edn. New York: Springer. The classical synthetic account.\n\nOn the topology of $\\mathbb{RP}^2$, see [topology-journey](https://github.com/cmsd2/topology-journey) Stage 1.06 and 2.02, and:\n\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2011) *Introduction to Topological Manifolds*. 2nd edn. New York: Springer. Chapter 3 for projective spaces as quotient manifolds.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
